{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"phys.org/Top ten plant and fungal species named new to science in 2023.txt"},"lines":{"text":"Scientists from the Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew together with international partners present their annual pick of the top 10 plants and fungi described as new to science in 2023.\n"},"line_number":8,"absolute_offset":192,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"2023"},"start":172,"end":176}]}}
{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"phys.org/Top ten plant and fungal species named new to science in 2023.txt"},"lines":{"text":"Approximately 74 plants and 15 fungi were named by RBG Kew scientists and partners in 2023, with species coming in from virtually every corner of the globe. These include the rocky outcrops of Antarctica, the unique ecosystems of Madagascar, and the Korean Peninsula. Unfortunately, many of these species are already considered threatened with extinction due to habitat clearance.\n"},"line_number":12,"absolute_offset":805,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"2023"},"start":86,"end":90}]}}
{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"phys.org/Top ten plant and fungal species named new to science in 2023.txt"},"lines":{"text":"According to recent findings published in Kew's State of the World's Plants and Fungi 2023 report, as many as 3 in 4 of the world's undescribed plant species are already threatened with extinction. By heading out into the field to collect, study, and describe previously unknown species, scientists hope to inform better conservation policies. In other words, in order to protect them, they first need to know what they're protecting.\n"},"line_number":16,"absolute_offset":1658,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"2023"},"start":86,"end":90}]}}
{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"phys.org/Top ten plant and fungal species named new to science in 2023.txt"},"lines":{"text":"Here are the top 10 weird and wonderful species named new to science in 2023:\n"},"line_number":28,"absolute_offset":3951,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"2023"},"start":72,"end":76}]}}
{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"phys.org/Top ten plant and fungal species named new to science in 2023.txt"},"lines":{"text":"At least three species of Lichtheimia are known to be human pathogens, meaning they can cause disease in humans, such as invasive mucormycosis in the lungs or in skin wounds. Described by Kew mycologist Dr. Paul Kirk, the new species Lichtheimia koreana was first isolated from soy waste in South Korea's Kunryang-ri, Cheongyang, and Chungnam Provinces in 2020 and 2023. Thankfully, it is not closely related to the three pathogenic species, and so scientists believe its pathogenic potential is limited.\n"},"line_number":48,"absolute_offset":6540,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"2023"},"start":365,"end":369}]}}
{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"phys.org/Top ten plant and fungal species named new to science in 2023.txt"},"lines":{"text":"In 2023, the scientists described a total of nine new species of Nicotiana, including N. olens, named after its pleasantly scented flowers. Hailing from New South Wales, the plant grows in open sites on clay or sandstone, with its type locality in Pines Picnic Grounds in Cocoparra National Park.\n"},"line_number":74,"absolute_offset":10648,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"2023"},"start":3,"end":7}]}}
{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"phys.org/Top ten plant and fungal species named new to science in 2023.txt"},"lines":{"text":"Dr. Brian Schrire, a Kew Honorary Research Associate, has been researching this genus for decades, publishing 80 new species, 18 of which are from South Africa and were described with co-authors this year—far exceeding the output of any other RBG Kew taxonomist in 2023. Among the newly described species is Indigofera abbottii, named after Anthony (Tony) Thomas Dixon Abbott, a pioneering conservationist and amateur plant collector who found innumerable plant species including this very namesake. Unfortunately, as is the case for many newly described species, it has been assessed as threatened due to the clearance of its habitat for agriculture and housing development.\n"},"line_number":90,"absolute_offset":13034,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"2023"},"start":267,"end":271}]}}
{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"phys.org/Top ten plant and fungal species named new to science in 2023.txt"},"lines":{"text":"The species' description was co-authored by RBG Kew's Dr. Iain Darbyshire, a specialist in the order Lamiales in Africa. This is his ninth co-published new species to science for 2023.\n"},"line_number":98,"absolute_offset":14640,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"2023"},"start":179,"end":183}]}}
